# Documentation Rules

Applies to everything under `docs/src/`. Mechanics are in `docs/AGENTS.md`; structure is in
`.conventions/CONVENTIONS.md`.

## Voice

- Direct and concrete. Short sentences. The reader is a contributor scanning for one fact.
- Present tense: "The engine holds the node", not "will hold".
- Second person for instructions ("run the self-test"), impersonal for descriptions ("the tree
  keeps the parent link").
- State limitations plainly and move on. No hedging ("simply", "just", "easily"), no promotional
  language ("powerful", "seamless"), no meta-commentary ("the honest take is", "it is worth
  noting").
- No em dash chains and no filler ("entirely", "crucially", "delve", "landscape"). If a sentence
  loses nothing when a word is removed, remove it.

## Evidence

- Every claim about the legacy project or an external framework carries a citation (`path:line`,
  spec URL, crate version) or an `[unverified]` marker.
- Every number carries its conditions: build profile, sample size, machine.
- "Fast" or "slow" without a measurement is not a statement.

## Page patterns

### Design document

1. **Problem** — what cannot be expressed today, with a failing example.
2. **Comparison** — how mature frameworks solve it, with sources.
3. **Contract** — the API, its invariants, and what it deliberately does not do.
4. **Implementation sites** — a table of file and responsibility.
5. **Corrections** — how this subsystem got here; see below.
6. **Verification** — tests by name, and what each would catch.
7. **Status** — what is implemented, what is planned.

### Corrections

Corrections are not a page. They are a `## Corrections` section inside the design document that owns
the contract, written as prose.

- One paragraph per correction, in the order it was found: what we believed, what was observed
  instead (the input, pixel, or measurement), what changed, and what catches it now.
- Name the test that pins it. If nothing pins it yet, say so and add the test.
- `###` sub-headings group corrections by contract area once the section passes a screen. Never
  turn it into a table of rows or a bullet dump.
- The Contract is current truth; Corrections is how we arrived. A correction that changes a
  contract statement updates the Contract in the same edit, and the paragraph says what changed.
- No "lesson learned" framing, no severity labels, no dates in the heading.

### Reference

Definition, how to use it, an options table, examples. Keep prose out of table cells.

## Formatting

- Wrap prose at 80 columns. Leave tables and links unwrapped when wrapping would break them.
- One command per `sh` block, no `$` prompt, no interleaved output.
- Prefer a table over a bullet list when every item has the same two or three fields.
- Bold the first word of a callout (`> **Note:**`), never a whole sentence.

## Terminology

| Use | Instead of |
| --- | --- |
| frame | tick, repaint (a frame is one build-and-present cycle) |
| declaration | view tree (a `Decl` is built per frame; the view is the app state) |
| control | widget, component (when naming a single MD3, Fluent, or Liquid Glass control) |
| control set | library, toolkit, design system (when naming all controls of one system) |
| legacy project | old code, v1, crabsnap (the pre-rewrite project) |
| nested session | test compositor, headless run (the scripted graphical session) |
| engine | framework, core (the layout, text, and drawing crates) |

## Status vocabulary

Use the same words everywhere:

- **Implemented** — the behavior exists and a test or screenshot proves it.
- **Partial** — part of the contract works; say which part is missing.
- **Planned** — designed, not built.
- **Unverified** — claimed or designed, not checked.

Do not use checkmarks or emoji as status; a word is searchable.
